„­bible_version£KJV¤book+§chapter¨sections—…¤book+§chapter«first_verseªlast_verse¦verses•„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙdNow when Festus was come into the province, after three days he ascended from CÃ¦sarea to Jerusalem.¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙ[Then the high priest and the chief of the Jews informed him against Paul, and besought him,¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙland desired favour against him, that he would send for him to Jerusalem, laying wait in the way to kill him.¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙlBut Festus answered, that Paul should be kept at CÃ¦sarea, and that he himself would depart shortly thither.¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙLet them therefore, said he, which among you are able, go down with me, and accuse this man, if there be any wickedness in him.¦layout ªlast_verse…¤book+§chapter«first_verseªlast_verse¦verses“„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙAnd when he had tarried among them more than ten days, he went down unto CÃ¦sarea; and the next day sitting on the judgment seat commanded Paul to be brought.¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙ And when he was come, the Jews which came down from Jerusalem stood round about, and laid many and grievous complaints against Paul, which they could not prove.¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙ™While he answered for himself, Neither against the law of the Jews, neither against the temple, nor yet against CÃ¦sar, have I offended any thing at all.¦layout ªlast_verse…¤book+§chapter«first_verse	ªlast_verse¦verses”„¥verse	¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙ”But Festus, willing to do the Jews a pleasure, answered Paul, and said, Wilt thou go up to Jerusalem, and there be judged of these things before me?¦layout ªlast_verse	„¥verse
¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙ‹Then said Paul, I stand at CÃ¦sar's judgment seat, where I ought to be judged: to the Jews have I done no wrong, as thou very well knowest.¦layout ªlast_verse
„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙÏFor if I be an offender, or have committed any thing worthy of death, I refuse not to die: but if there be none of these things whereof these accuse me, no man may deliver me unto them. I appeal unto CÃ¦sar.¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙyThen Festus, when he had conferred with the council, answered, Hast thou appealed unto CÃ¦sar? unto CÃ¦sar shalt thou go.¦layout ªlast_verse…¤book+§chapter«first_verseªlast_verse¦verses•„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙTAnd after certain days king Agrippa and Bernice came unto CÃ¦sarea to salute Festus.¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙŠAnd when they had been there many days, Festus declared Paul's cause unto the king, saying, There is a certain man left in bonds by Felix:¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙ…about whom, when I was at Jerusalem, the chief priests and the elders of the Jews informed me, desiring to have judgment against him.¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙßTo whom I answered, It is not the manner of the Romans to deliver any man to die, before that he which is accused have the accusers face to face, and have licence to answer for himself concerning the crime laid against him.¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙTherefore, when they were come hither, without any delay on the morrow I sat on the judgment seat, and commanded the man to be brought forth.¦layout ªlast_verse…¤book+§chapter«first_verseªlast_verse¦verses”„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙcAgainst whom when the accusers stood up, they brought none accusation of such things as I supposed:¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙ‚but had certain questions against him of their own superstition, and of one Jesus, which was dead, whom Paul affirmed to be alive.¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙ†And because I doubted of such manner of questions, I asked him whether he would go to Jerusalem, and there be judged of these matters.¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙƒBut when Paul had appealed to be reserved unto the hearing of Augustus, I commanded him to be kept till I might send him to CÃ¦sar.¦layout ªlast_verse…¤book+§chapter«first_verseªlast_verse¦verses‘„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙiThen Agrippa said unto Festus, I would also hear the man myself. To morrow, said he, thou shalt hear him.¦layout ªlast_verse…¤book+§chapter«first_verseªlast_verse¦verses•„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙÙAnd on the morrow, when Agrippa was come, and Bernice, with great pomp, and was entered into the place of hearing, with the chief captains, and principal men of the city, at Festus' commandment Paul was brought forth.¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙçAnd Festus said, King Agrippa, and all men which are here present with us, ye see this man, about whom all the multitude of the Jews have dealt with me, both at Jerusalem, and also here, crying that he ought not to live any longer.¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙBut when I found that he had committed nothing worthy of death, and that he himself hath appealed to Augustus, I have determined to send him.¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙÍOf whom I have no certain thing to write unto my lord. Wherefore I have brought him forth before you, and specially before thee, O king Agrippa, that, after examination had, I might have somewhat to write.¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙlFor it seemeth to me unreasonable to send a prisoner, and not withal to signify the crimes laid against him.¦layout ªlast_verse